## The Bug

The manual expiry check uses strict less-than (`<`), so when `decoded.exp === Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)`, the condition is false and the expired token is accepted.

But there's a deeper issue: **`jwt.verify` already validates expiry** and throws `TokenExpiredError` when `exp <= now`. The manual check is therefore redundant *and* buggy — it runs only on tokens that already passed `jwt.verify`, creating a logic gap.

The fix is to remove the manual check entirely and handle `TokenExpiredError` specifically in the catch block:

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }

  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = decoded;
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    // Unexpected errors (e.g. missing JWT_SECRET) should not leak details
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Authentication error' });
  }
}

module.exports = authMiddleware;
```

**What changed and why:**

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Expiry check | Manual `decoded.exp < now` (misses `===`) | Delegated to `jwt.verify` (uses `<=`) |
| Expired vs invalid | Same 401 message | Distinct messages for debugging |
| Unexpected errors | Swallowed as "Invalid token" | Returns 500, doesn't leak internals |

`jwt.verify` uses `exp <= now` (rejects at exactly expiry), which is the correct semantics per [RFC 7519 §4.1.4](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7519#section-4.1.4).
